{"subjectId":"java","id":"set-implementations","title":"Set: HashSet, LinkedHashSet & TreeSet","group":"Collections Framework","order":43,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A <code>Set</code> stores each element at most once. The three implementations differ only in <em>ordering</em> and <em>speed</em>: hash (no order), linked hash (insertion order), tree (sorted).</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Set</strong> — a collection with no duplicate elements — <code>add</code> returns <code>false</code> if an <em>equal</em> element is already present. “Equal” means <code>equals</code>/<code>hashCode</code> for hash sets and <code>compareTo</code>/<code>compare</code> == 0 for <code>TreeSet</code>.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-table\"></i>Comparison</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>HashSet</code></th><th><code>LinkedHashSet</code></th><th><code>TreeSet</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Backed by</td><td>a <code>HashMap</code> (elements are keys)</td><td>a <code>LinkedHashMap</code></td><td>a <code>TreeMap</code> (red-black tree)</td></tr><tr><td>Iteration order</td><td>Unspecified</td><td>Insertion order</td><td>Sorted (natural or <code>Comparator</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>add</code> / <code>remove</code> / <code>contains</code></td><td>O(1) average</td><td>O(1) average</td><td>O(log n)</td></tr><tr><td><code>null</code> element</td><td>One allowed</td><td>One allowed</td><td>No (<code>NullPointerException</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>Needs</td><td><code>equals</code> + <code>hashCode</code></td><td><code>equals</code> + <code>hashCode</code></td><td><code>Comparable</code> or a <code>Comparator</code></td></tr><tr><td>Extra API</td><td>—</td><td><code>reversed()</code>, <code>getFirst()</code> (Java 21)</td><td><code>first</code>, <code>floor</code>, <code>ceiling</code>, <code>headSet</code>, <code>subSet</code>…</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-sort\"></i>Same input, three orders</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List&lt;Integer&gt; input = List.of(42, 7, 19, 7, 3, 42);\n        System.out.println(\"HashSet       \" + new HashSet&lt;&gt;(input));\n        System.out.println(\"LinkedHashSet \" + new LinkedHashSet&lt;&gt;(input));\n        System.out.println(\"TreeSet       \" + new TreeSet&lt;&gt;(input));\n\n        Set&lt;String&gt; seen = new HashSet&lt;&gt;();\n        System.out.println(seen.add(\"x\") + \" \" + seen.add(\"x\"));   // second add is rejected\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>HashSet       [19, 3, 7, 42]\nLinkedHashSet [42, 7, 19, 3]\nTreeSet       [3, 7, 19, 42]\ntrue false</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>HashSet</code>'s order above comes from the bucket index (<code>hash &amp; (capacity − 1)</code>: 42→10, 7→7, 19→3, 3→3 with 16 buckets). It is an implementation detail that changes as the set resizes — never depend on it.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-binary-tree\"></i><code>TreeSet</code> as a <code>NavigableSet</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        TreeSet&lt;Integer&gt; ts = new TreeSet&lt;&gt;(List.of(10, 20, 30, 40, 50));\n        System.out.println(ts.first() + \" \" + ts.last());\n        System.out.println(ts.floor(25) + \" \" + ts.ceiling(25));   // &lt;= 25, &gt;= 25\n        System.out.println(ts.lower(20) + \" \" + ts.higher(20));    // &lt; 20, &gt; 20\n        System.out.println(ts.floor(5));                           // none -&gt; null\n        System.out.println(ts.headSet(30) + \" \" + ts.tailSet(30)); // [..30) and [30..]\n        System.out.println(ts.subSet(20, true, 40, true));\n        System.out.println(ts.descendingSet());\n        System.out.println(ts.pollFirst() + \" -&gt; \" + ts);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>10 50\n20 30\n10 30\nnull\n[10, 20] [30, 40, 50]\n[20, 30, 40]\n[50, 40, 30, 20, 10]\n10 -&gt; [20, 30, 40, 50]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Trap: forgetting <code>equals</code>/<code>hashCode</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Set&lt;Point&gt; raw = new HashSet&lt;&gt;();\n        raw.add(new Point(1, 2));\n        raw.add(new Point(1, 2));\n        System.out.println(\"class without equals/hashCode: \" + raw.size());\n\n        Set&lt;PointRec&gt; rec = new HashSet&lt;&gt;();\n        rec.add(new PointRec(1, 2));\n        rec.add(new PointRec(1, 2));\n        System.out.println(\"record: \" + rec.size());\n    }\n}\n\nclass Point {                        // uses Object's identity equals/hashCode\n    final int x, y;\n    Point(int x, int y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; }\n}\n\nrecord PointRec(int x, int y) {}     // records generate equals/hashCode from the fields</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>class without equals/hashCode: 2\nrecord: 1</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>TreeSet</code> ignores <code>equals</code> entirely and treats <code>compare(a, b) == 0</code> as “duplicate”. A comparator that only compares by, say, length will silently drop <code>\"cat\"</code> when <code>\"dog\"</code> is already present.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Set&lt;String&gt; byLength = new TreeSet&lt;&gt;(Comparator.comparingInt(String::length));\n        byLength.addAll(List.of(\"dog\", \"cat\", \"horse\"));\n        System.out.println(byLength);       // \"cat\" looked like a duplicate of \"dog\"\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[dog, horse]</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How does HashSet guarantee uniqueness?</summary><div><p>It is a wrapper around a <code>HashMap</code> whose keys are the elements and whose values are one shared dummy <code>PRESENT</code> object. <code>add(e)</code> is <code>map.put(e, PRESENT) == null</code> — so uniqueness is exactly <code>HashMap</code> key uniqueness: same bucket via <code>hashCode</code>, then <code>equals</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>HashSet vs TreeSet — which and why?</summary><div><p><code>HashSet</code> for fast O(1) membership when order doesn't matter. <code>TreeSet</code> when you need sorted iteration or range/nearest queries (<code>floor</code>, <code>ceiling</code>, <code>subSet</code>) at O(log n), and it requires comparable elements and rejects <code>null</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Can you add null to a TreeSet?</summary><div><p>Not with natural ordering — <code>compareTo</code> on <code>null</code> throws <code>NullPointerException</code>. It works only with a comparator that handles nulls, e.g. <code>Comparator.nullsFirst(Comparator.naturalOrder())</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}